@prefix glosis_cl: <http://w3id.org/glosis/model/codelists/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-ForestAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Forest Abundance Property refers to the proportion or extent of land covered by forest vegetation in a given area. It is often quantified as the percentage of land area dominated by tree species, reflecting the ecological importance of forests in influencing soil fertility, moisture retention, and biodiversity. Forested areas typically contribute to soil formation and stabilization through leaf litter and root systems that reduce erosion. Source: FAO, 'Forest Resources Assessment'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "ForestAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "FForest Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-GrassAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Grass Abundance Property indicates the extent or percentage of land covered by grasses or herbaceous plants. This property is important for understanding land productivity, grazing capacity, and soil health, as grasses play a key role in preventing soil erosion, enhancing organic matter content, and maintaining soil structure. Source: FAO, 'Grassland Ecosystems and Management'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "GrassAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "GGrass Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-MajorLandFormProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Major Land Form Property refers to the significant physical features of the land, such as mountains, valleys, and plateaus, that influence soil development and landscape processes. These landforms shape the flow of water, sediment transport, and nutrient cycling, directly affecting the types of soils that form in different regions. Source: FAO, 'World Soil Resources Report'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "MajorLandFormProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "MMajor Land Form Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-ParentDepositionProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Parent Deposition Property refers to the accumulation of materials, such as sediments or volcanic ash, that contribute to soil formation. These parent materials are deposited through natural processes such as erosion, weathering, or sediment transport, influencing soil texture, nutrient content, and water retention properties. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Soil Genesis and Classification'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "ParentDepositionProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "PParent Deposition Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-ParticleSizeFractionsSumProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Particle Size Fractions Sum refers to the total proportion of sand, silt, and clay particles in soil, expressed as a percentage. It ensures the sum of individual particle size fractions adds up to 100%, a check for accurate soil texture analysis. Particle size distribution impacts water retention, permeability, and soil structure. Source: Hillel, D. (2004). Introduction to Environmental Soil Physics." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "ParticleSizeFractionsSumProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "PParticle Size Fractions Sum Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-PhysiographyProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Physiography Property describes the physical characteristics of a landscape, including its landforms, slopes, and elevation. These features influence soil properties such as drainage, erosion, and moisture retention, helping to determine soil suitability for various land uses. Source: FAO, 'Soil Geography and Mapping'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "PhysiographyProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "PPhysiography Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-RootsPresenceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Roots Presence Property identifies whether roots are visible in a soil profile or horizon. It indicates biological activity, soil health, and the suitability of the soil for plant growth. Roots are commonly observed during soil surveys and are classified by type and density. Source: Soil Survey Staff (2014). Keys to Soil Taxonomy." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "RootsPresenceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "RRoots Presence Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-SaltThicknessProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Salt thickness refers to the depth of the soil profile at which soluble salts are present in significant concentrations. This property is particularly important in regions subject to evaporation and capillary rise, where salts accumulate near the surface or at specific depths. Salt thickness can impact soil fertility and water retention, as the concentrated salts can inhibit root growth and water uptake. This property is commonly determined through soil sampling and electrical conductivity measurements at various depths. Source: United States Salinity Laboratory Staff. (1954). Diagnosis and Improvement of Saline and Alkali Soils. U.S. Department of Agriculture Handbook No. 60. " ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "SaltThicknessProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "SSalt Thickness Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-VegetationClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Vegetation Class Property refers to the categorization of vegetation types based on species composition and structure, such as forests, grasslands, and wetlands. The class of vegetation affects soil fertility, microbial activity, and water retention, which in turn influences agricultural potential and ecosystem services. Source: FAO, 'Vegetation and Land Use in Arid Zones'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "VegetationClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "VVegetation Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-VoidsClassificationProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Voids Classification Property categorizes voids or pores in the soil based on size, shape, and arrangement. These voids are critical for water movement, air circulation, and root penetration. They are classified into macropores (large), mesopores (medium), and micropores (small). Source: Hillel, D. (2004). Introduction to Environmental Soil Physics." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "VoidsClassificationProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "VVoids Classification Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-bareCoverAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bare Cover Abundance Property refers to the extent of land area without vegetation or artificial cover, such as bare soil or rocky surfaces. High proportions of bare cover increase the risk of soil erosion and desertification, particularly in areas with poor vegetation or human disturbance. Source: FAO, 'Land Use and Land Cover Classification'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "bareCoverAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bare Cover Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-biologicalAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Biological Abundance Property evaluates the presence and quantity of soil organisms, such as microbes, insects, and earthworms. This property reflects soil health, nutrient cycling, and organic matter decomposition, which are vital for maintaining soil fertility. Source: Lavelle, P., & Spain, A.V. (2001). Soil Ecology." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "biologicalAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Biological Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-biologicalFeaturesProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Biological Features Property examines qualitative aspects of soil biological activity, including the types of organisms present, their diversity, and their roles in soil ecosystems. It encompasses processes like nitrogen fixation, organic matter decomposition, and bioturbation. Source: Soil Science Society of America (SSSA) Glossary." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "biologicalFeaturesProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Biological Features Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-bleachedSandProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Bleached Sand property refers to the physical characteristic of sandy soil that has undergone a bleaching process due to leaching, where minerals such as iron and other elements have been removed by water. This process often occurs in areas with high rainfall, leading to a soil that appears lighter in color. Bleached sand typically indicates low fertility and poor nutrient-holding capacity due to the lack of iron and other nutrients that are washed away. Source: Brady, N. C., & Weil, R. R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils (14th ed.). Pearson Prentice Hall." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "bleachedSandProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bleached Sand Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-boundaryDistinctnessProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Boundary Distinctness Property describes the sharpness of transitions between soil horizons. Boundaries can range from abrupt (clear demarcation) to diffuse (gradual blending), providing insights into soil formation processes and profile development. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "boundaryDistinctnessProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Boundary Distinctness Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-boundaryTopographyProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Boundary Topography Property characterizes the shape of soil horizon boundaries, which may be smooth, wavy, irregular, or broken. It reflects processes like erosion, deposition, or biological activity that influence horizon development. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "boundaryTopographyProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Boundary Topography Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-bulkDensityMineralProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Bulk Density of Mineral Soil refers specifically to the density of the mineral portion of the soil, excluding organic matter. It provides insights into the physical state and compaction of mineral-dominated soils, influencing water movement and root growth. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "bulkDensityMineralProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bulk Density Mineral Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-bulkDensityPeatProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Bulk Density of Peat measures the mass of dry organic soil (peat) per unit volume. Peat soils typically have low bulk density due to their high organic matter content and porosity, which significantly affects their water retention and drainage characteristics. Source: Rydin, H., & Jeglum, J. (2006). The Biology of Peatlands." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "bulkDensityPeatProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bulk Density Peat Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-carbonatesContentProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Carbonates Content Property quantifies the concentration of carbonate minerals (e.g., CaCO₃, MgCO₃) in soil. These compounds influence soil pH, fertility, and structure, and are prevalent in calcareous soils. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "carbonatesContentProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carbonates Content Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-carbonatesFormsProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Carbonates Forms Property describes the types of carbonate minerals present in soil, such as calcite, dolomite, or aragonite. These forms impact soil chemistry, fertility, and physical characteristics differently depending on their composition and solubility. Source: Sparks, D.L. (2003). Environmental Soil Chemistry." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "carbonatesFormsProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carbonates Forms Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cationExchangeCapacityEffectiveProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Effective Cation Exchange Capacity (ECEC) measures the ability of soil to hold and exchange cations at a given pH, often near field conditions. It provides insight into soil fertility, nutrient availability, and buffering capacity in acidic soils. Source: Soil Survey Staff (2014). Keys to Soil Taxonomy." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cationExchangeCapacityEffectiveProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cation Exchange Capacity Effective Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cationExchangeCapacityProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Cation Exchange Capacity (CEC) measures the soil's total capacity to hold and exchange positively charged ions (cations). It is influenced by clay and organic matter content and is a critical indicator of soil fertility and nutrient retention. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cationExchangeCapacityProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cation Exchange Capacity Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cationsSumProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Cations Sum Property calculates the total concentration of exchangeable cations (e.g., Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, K⁺, Na⁺) in the soil. It helps determine soil fertility and salinity and is commonly expressed in milliequivalents per 100 grams of soil. Source: Soil Science Society of America (SSSA) Glossary." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cationsSumProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cations Sum Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cementationContinuityProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Cementation Continuity Property describes the extent to which soil particles are bound together by cementing agents, such as calcium carbonate, iron oxides, or silica. Cementation affects soil permeability, root penetration, and the development of soil horizons. Continuity can be classified as continuous, discontinuous, or patchy. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cementationContinuityProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cementation Continuity Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cementationDegreeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Cementation Degree Property measures how strongly soil particles are bonded by cementing agents. It ranges from weakly cemented (friable) to strongly cemented (hard). This property influences soil strength and its suitability for agricultural and engineering purposes. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cementationDegreeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cementation Degree Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cementationFabricProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Cementation Fabric Property examines the texture, structure, and arrangement of cementing materials within the soil. It provides insights into soil formation processes and the mechanical stability of the soil matrix. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cementationFabricProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cementation Fabric Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cementationNatureProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Cementation Nature Property identifies the specific type of material responsible for binding soil particles, such as carbonates, silica, or iron oxides. Understanding this helps in soil classification and predicting behavior under varying environmental conditions. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cementationNatureProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cementation Nature Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-coatingAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Coating Abundance Property evaluates the quantity of coatings on soil particles, such as organic matter, clay, or iron oxides. Coatings influence soil color, nutrient availability, and water movement. The abundance is typically classified as few, common, or many. Source: Soil Survey Staff (2014). Keys to Soil Taxonomy." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "coatingAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coating Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-coatingContrastProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Coating Contrast Property assesses the visual distinction between coatings on soil particles and the underlying material. High contrast indicates significant differences in composition or color, providing clues about soil processes like illuviation or biological activity. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "coatingContrastProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coating Contrast Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-coatingFormProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Coating Form Property describes the shape and distribution of coatings on soil particles, such as patchy, continuous, or layered. These forms are indicative of soil genesis and chemical weathering processes. Source: Soil Science Society of America (SSSA) Glossary." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "coatingFormProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coating Form Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-coatingLocationProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Coating Location Property identifies where coatings are found within the soil profile, such as along root channels, pore spaces, or on particle Layer-Horizons. This property helps determine the movement of water and nutrients in the soil. Source: Soil Survey Staff (2014). Keys to Soil Taxonomy." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "coatingLocationProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coating Location Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-coatingNatureProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Coating Nature Property examines the composition of soil coatings, such as organic, clay, or iron-rich coatings. The nature of these coatings can influence nutrient retention, soil color, and water dynamics. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "coatingNatureProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coating Nature Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-colourDryProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Colour Dry property describes the color of soil when it is in a dry state. Soil color in the dry condition is influenced by the soil’s mineral composition and moisture content. For example, soils with high organic matter content typically appear dark brown or black when dry, while sandy or saline soils may appear lighter in color. Dry soil color is used in soil classification and can provide insights into the soil's composition and drainage. Source: Soil Science Society of America. (2008). Soil Science Glossary. Soil Science Society of America." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "colourDryProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Colour Dry Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-colourMoistProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Colour Moist property refers to the soil's color when it is in a wet or moist condition. Moisture enhances the color intensity of the soil, which is often darker compared to its dry state. This is particularly helpful in determining organic matter content, iron oxide presence, and soil moisture retention. For instance, moist soils with abundant organic material tend to appear darker, whereas more mineral-rich soils can appear lighter in color. Source: Soil Science Society of America. (2008). Soil Science Glossary. Soil Science Society of America." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "colourMoistProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Colour Moist Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-consistenceDryProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Consistence Dry Property evaluates the soil's behavior when dry, including its resistance to deformation or crumbling. It ranges from loose to hard and provides information about soil compaction and management practices. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "consistenceDryProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Consistence Dry Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-consistenceMoistProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Consistence Moist Property assesses how soil responds to pressure when moist. Categories include friable, firm, or plastic. This property is essential for understanding soil workability and water retention. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "consistenceMoistProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Consistence Moist Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cracksDepthProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Cracks Depth property refers to the measurement of the vertical depth of cracks formed in soil, which is typically observed in dry conditions when the soil shrinks. Cracks are more common in clayey soils that experience significant shrinkage upon drying. The depth of these cracks can indicate soil moisture levels, compaction, and the ability of the soil to expand and contract, which affects water infiltration and root penetration. Source: McSweeney, K. (2009). Soil and Water Conservation Handbook. CRC Press." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cracksDepthProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cracks Depth Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cracksDistanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Cracks Distance property refers to the horizontal spacing between cracks in the soil Layer-Horizon, which can occur due to the shrinkage of soil as it dries. Cracks form when water content decreases, and their distance is an indicator of the soil's shrink-swell behavior. Larger distances between cracks generally indicate more severe soil shrinkage, which can affect root growth and water infiltration. Source: McSweeney, K. (2009). Soil and Water Conservation Handbook. CRC Press.",
        "The Cracks Distance property refers to the horizontal spacing between cracks in the soil surface, which can occur due to the shrinkage of soil as it dries. Cracks form when water content decreases, and their distance is an indicator of the soil's shrink-swell behavior. Larger distances between cracks generally indicate more severe soil shrinkage, which can affect root growth and water infiltration. Source: McSweeney, K. (2009). Soil and Water Conservation Handbook. CRC Press." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cracksDistanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cracks Distance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cracksWidthProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Cracks Width property refers to the measurement of the gap or space between the sides of soil cracks. The width of these cracks is typically influenced by soil moisture content and texture. Wider cracks are indicative of drier conditions and are commonly found in soils with high clay content, which tend to shrink and form deep cracks when the moisture content is reduced. Source: McSweeney, K. (2009). Soil and Water Conservation Handbook. CRC Press." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cracksWidthProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cracks Width Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-cropClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Crop Class Property refers to the categorization of land based on the type of crops cultivated. This property is used to assess soil management practices and land productivity, as different crops require specific soil conditions for optimal growth. Source: FAO, 'Guidelines for Soil Profile Description'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "cropClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crop Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-dryConsistencyProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Dry Consistency Property is synonymous with the Consistence Dry Property, focusing on the soil's resistance to breaking apart or deforming when dry. It indicates the soil's mechanical strength and susceptibility to erosion. Source: Soil Survey Staff (2014). Keys to Soil Taxonomy." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "dryConsistencyProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dry Consistency Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-erosionActivityPeriodProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Erosion Activity Period Property refers to the time frame during which soil erosion processes, such as wind or water erosion, are most active in a given area. This period is influenced by climatic conditions, seasonal rainfall, and land use, and it helps in assessing the vulnerability of soils to degradation. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Soil Erosion and Conservation'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "erosionActivityPeriodProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Erosion Activity Period Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-erosionAreaAffectedProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Erosion Area Affected Property refers to the extent of land area that has been impacted by erosion processes, such as water or wind erosion. This property is important for evaluating the severity of soil degradation in a given region and helps in identifying areas that require soil conservation measures. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Soil Erosion Research and Technology Transfer'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "erosionAreaAffectedProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Erosion Area Affected Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-erosionCategoryProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Erosion Category Property classifies the type and severity of erosion in a given area. Categories are often based on the nature of the erosive forces (e.g., water, wind) and the degree of soil loss, ranging from slight to severe. This classification aids in prioritizing conservation efforts and understanding the potential impacts on soil quality. Source: FAO, 'Soil Erosion and Conservation'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "erosionCategoryProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Erosion Category Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-erosionDegreeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Erosion Degree Property refers to the level of intensity or severity of soil erosion in an area, categorized into classes such as slight, moderate, or severe. The degree of erosion helps in assessing the extent of soil degradation and informs decisions on soil conservation and land management. Source: FAO, 'Soil Erosion and Conservation'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "erosionDegreeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Erosion Degree Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-erosionTotalAreaAffectedProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Erosion Total Area Affected Property quantifies the total land area affected by erosion processes. It is an important measure in understanding the broader impact of erosion on soil health, productivity, and ecosystem services across a landscape. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Soil Erosion and Landscape Analysis'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "erosionTotalAreaAffectedProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Erosion Total Area Affected Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-floodDurationProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Flood Duration Property refers to the length of time during which an area is inundated by floodwaters. The duration of flooding can significantly affect soil structure, nutrient availability, and plant growth, especially in flood-prone regions. Source: USGS, 'Floodplain Management'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "floodDurationProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Flood Duration Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-floodFrequencyProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Flood Frequency Property refers to how often flooding occurs in a particular area, typically expressed in terms of recurrence intervals (e.g., once every 10 years). This property is vital for assessing flood risk, soil erosion, and the potential for soil nutrient loss due to repeated flooding events. Source: USGS, 'Floodplain Management'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "floodFrequencyProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Flood Frequency Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-fragmentCoverProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Fragment Cover property describes the proportion of the soil Layer-Horizon covered by coarse fragments such as rocks, stones, or other large particles. This property is important because a high fragment cover can reduce soil erosion but may also interfere with water infiltration and root growth. Fragment cover is often used as an indicator of soil stability and erosion potential. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description (4th ed.). Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations.",
        "The Fragment Cover property describes the proportion of the soil surface covered by coarse fragments such as rocks, stones, or other large particles. This property is important because a high fragment cover can reduce soil erosion but may also interfere with water infiltration and root growth. Fragment cover is often used as an indicator of soil stability and erosion potential. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description (4th ed.). Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "fragmentCoverProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fragment Cover Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-fragmentsClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Fragments Class Property categorizes the size, shape, and quantity of coarse fragments (e.g., gravel, stones) present in the soil. These fragments affect soil porosity, water retention, and root penetration. The classification includes gravelly, stony, or bouldery soils. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "fragmentsClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fragments Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-fragmentsSizeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Fragments Size property refers to the dimensions of rock or mineral fragments present in the soil. These fragments are categorized based on their size, ranging from small gravel particles to larger stones and boulders. Larger fragments tend to reduce soil fertility, inhibit root growth, and decrease water retention. Smaller fragments, in contrast, contribute to soil texture and permeability. Source: Soil Science Society of America. (2008). Soil Science Glossary. Soil Science Society of America." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "fragmentsSizeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fragments Size Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-geologyProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Geology Property refers to the underlying geological composition of an area, including rock types, mineral content, and geological processes. The geology of a region influences soil formation, structure, fertility, and drainage properties. Source: AGI, 'Encyclopedia of Geology'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "geologyProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Geology Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-groundwaterDepthProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Groundwater Depth Property indicates the depth from the soil surface to the water table, which affects soil moisture levels, plant growth, and root penetration. Shallow groundwater can influence soil salinity and nutrient availability, while deep groundwater may limit water access for plants. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Groundwater and Soil'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "groundwaterDepthProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Groundwater Depth Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-gypsumContentProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Gypsum Content Property quantifies the amount of gypsum (calcium sulfate dihydrate) present in the soil. Gypsum affects soil structure, water infiltration, and nutrient availability, especially in arid and semi-arid regions. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "gypsumContentProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gypsum Content Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-gypsumFormsProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Gypsum Forms Property describes the morphology of gypsum within the soil, such as crystals, veins, or nodules. The form indicates the processes of soil formation and water movement in gypsum-rich soils. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "gypsumFormsProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gypsum Forms Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-gypsumWeightProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Gypsum Weight Property measures the mass of gypsum in the soil profile. This value helps evaluate the potential for soil salinity issues and influences decisions for agricultural or land reclamation practices. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "gypsumWeightProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gypsum Weight Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-humanInfluenceClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Human Influence Class Property classifies the extent of human impact on a landscape or ecosystem, ranging from minimal disturbance to high levels of modification. This classification helps in understanding the effects of urbanization, agriculture, or industrial activity on soil properties and ecosystem functions. Source: FAO, 'Land Degradation Assessment in Drylands'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "humanInfluenceClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Human Influence Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-infiltrationRateClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Infiltration Rate Class property classifies the soil’s ability to absorb water based on observed infiltration rates. This classification is typically divided into categories like slow, moderate, or rapid infiltration, based on how quickly water is absorbed into the soil. It is an essential property for understanding drainage, runoff, and irrigation needs. Source: Hillel, D. (2004). Introduction to Soil Physics. Academic Press." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "infiltrationRateClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Infiltration Rate Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-infiltrationRateNumericProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Infiltration Rate Numeric property provides a quantitative measure of the rate at which water enters the soil, usually expressed in units such as millimeters per hour (mm/h). This numeric value is essential for determining soil permeability, irrigation management, and runoff prediction. Higher infiltration rates indicate well-drained soils, while lower rates suggest compacted or clay-rich soils. Source: Bouwer, H. (2000). Integrated Water Management for the 21st Century. Water Resources Research." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "infiltrationRateNumericProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Infiltration Rate Numeric Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-koeppenClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Koeppen Class Property refers to the classification of climate zones based on temperature and precipitation patterns, as defined by the Köppen climate classification system. This classification directly influences soil temperature, moisture content, and vegetation types, which in turn affect soil formation and properties. Source: Köppen, W., 'Geiger's Climate Maps'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "koeppenClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Koeppen Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-landUseClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Land Use Class Property refers to the classification of land based on its primary use, such as agricultural, industrial, urban, or natural areas. This property helps assess the impact of land management practices on soil quality, including erosion risks, compaction, and fertility, and is critical for sustainable land-use planning. Source: FAO, 'Land Use and Land Cover Classification'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "landUseClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Land Use Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-landformComplexProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Landform Complex Property describes a region with multiple interrelated landforms such as ridges, valleys, and hills. This property is essential for understanding how diverse landforms within a landscape influence soil distribution, water flow, and erosion patterns, ultimately affecting soil management strategies. Source: FAO, 'Soil Formation and Classification'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "landformComplexProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Landform Complex Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-lithologyProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Lithology Property refers to the physical characteristics of the parent material, such as the mineral composition, texture, and structure of the underlying rocks or sediments. The lithology of an area influences soil formation by affecting drainage, nutrient content, and the ability of the soil to support vegetation. Source: AGI, 'Encyclopedia of Geology'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "lithologyProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lithology Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mineralConcAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mineral Concentration Abundance Property evaluates the amount of specific minerals, such as quartz, feldspar, or mica, in the soil. This property provides insights into soil parent material and fertility. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mineralConcAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineral Conc Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mineralConcColourProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mineral Concentration Colour Property describes the color of concentrated mineral deposits in the soil, such as iron oxides (red) or manganese nodules (black). It helps identify soil processes like oxidation or leaching. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mineralConcColourProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineral Conc Colour Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mineralConcHardnessProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mineral Concentration Hardness Property assesses the physical hardness of mineral deposits in soil. Hardness influences soil strength and resistance to erosion or mechanical disruption. Source: Sparks, D.L. (2003). Environmental Soil Chemistry." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mineralConcHardnessProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineral Conc Hardness Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mineralConcKindProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mineral Concentration Kind Property identifies the specific type of mineral concentrations found in soil, such as gypsum, calcite, or hematite. This classification aids in understanding soil genesis and chemistry. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mineralConcKindProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineral Conc Kind Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mineralConcNatureProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mineral Concentration Nature Property examines the origin and composition of concentrated minerals in soil. It provides clues about weathering, leaching, and other pedogenic processes. Source: Soil Survey Staff (2014). Keys to Soil Taxonomy." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mineralConcNatureProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineral Conc Nature Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mineralConcShapeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mineral Concentration Shape Property describes the physical shape of mineral accumulations, such as rounded, angular, or irregular. These shapes help infer soil formation processes and transport history. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mineralConcShapeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineral Conc Shape Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mineralConcSizeeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mineral Concentration Size Property measures the dimensions of concentrated mineral deposits in soil, typically expressed in millimeters. Size categories range from small grains to large nodules or concretions. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mineralConcSizeeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineral Conc Sizee Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mineralConcVolumeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mineral Concentration Volume Property quantifies the proportion of soil volume occupied by mineral concentrations. It is used to assess the impact of these materials on soil structure and permeability. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mineralConcVolumeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineral Conc Volume Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mineralContentProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mineral Content Property evaluates the overall abundance of mineral particles in soil, including sand, silt, and clay fractions. It provides essential information about soil texture, fertility, and parent material. Source: Hillel, D. (2004). Introduction to Environmental Soil Physics." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mineralContentProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineral Content Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mineralFragmentsProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mineral Fragments Property refers to the presence and characteristics of coarse mineral particles (e.g., gravel, pebbles, stones) in the soil. These fragments influence soil drainage, root penetration, and overall physical structure. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mineralFragmentsProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineral Fragments Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-moistConsistencyProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Moist Consistency Property evaluates the soil's behavior under pressure when moist. Categories include friable, firm, or plastic. It provides insights into soil workability and its ability to support plant growth. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "moistConsistencyProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Moist Consistency Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-moistureContentProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Moisture Content Property measures the amount of water present in soil, expressed as a percentage of its dry weight. This property is critical for assessing soil water retention, irrigation needs, and plant-available water. Source: Hillel, D. (2004). Introduction to Environmental Soil Physics." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "moistureContentProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Moisture Content Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mottlesAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mottles Abundance Property describes the frequency of mottling (spots of contrasting color) in soil horizons. Mottling indicates variations in soil aeration, drainage, or water saturation. Abundance is typically classified as few, common, or many. Source: Soil Survey Staff (2014). Keys to Soil Taxonomy." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mottlesAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mottles Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mottlesBoundaryClassificationProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mottles Boundary Classification Property assesses the sharpness and distinctness of boundaries between mottled and non-mottled areas within the soil. It provides clues about soil processes such as oxidation-reduction reactions. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mottlesBoundaryClassificationProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mottles Boundary Classification Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mottlesColourProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mottles Colour Property evaluates the color of soil mottles, often indicative of oxidation states of iron or manganese. For example, reddish colors suggest oxidation, while gray colors indicate reduction and poor drainage. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mottlesColourProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mottles Colour Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mottlesContrastProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mottles Contrast Property measures the visual distinction between mottles and the surrounding soil matrix. High contrast indicates stark differences, often linked to fluctuating water tables or drainage issues. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mottlesContrastProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mottles Contrast Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mottlesPresenceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mottles Presence Property refers to the existence of mottles in soil, often signaling periodic water saturation, drainage problems, or fluctuating redox conditions. This property is essential for wetland delineation and soil health assessment. Source: Soil Survey Staff (2014). Keys to Soil Taxonomy." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mottlesPresenceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mottles Presence Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-mottlesSizeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mottles Size Property describes the dimensions of soil mottles, typically classified as fine, medium, or coarse. The size reflects the intensity and scale of soil processes such as oxidation and reduction. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "mottlesSizeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mottles Size Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-oxalateExtractableOpticalDensityProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Oxalate Extractable Optical Density Property measures the concentration of poorly crystalline forms of iron, aluminum, and organic matter in soil, often associated with volcanic soils. It is determined using ammonium oxalate extraction. Source: Sparks, D.L. (2003). Environmental Soil Chemistry." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "oxalateExtractableOpticalDensityProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Oxalate Extractable Optical Density Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-parentLithologyProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Parent Lithology Property is the specific geological composition of the parent material (e.g., bedrock or sediment) from which the soil develops. The mineral content and texture of this material directly affect the physical and chemical properties of the soil, such as fertility, permeability, and soil depth. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Soil Genesis and Classification'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "parentLithologyProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Parent Lithology Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-peatDecompostionProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Peat Decomposition Property evaluates the degree of decomposition in organic soils or peat. Categories include fibric (least decomposed), hemic (moderately decomposed), and sapric (highly decomposed), which influence soil fertility and drainage. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "peatDecompostionProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peat Decompostion Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-peatDrainageProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Peat Drainage Property assesses the water removal capacity in peat soils, essential for determining their suitability for agriculture, conservation, or other uses. Poorly drained peat often accumulates organic material due to low decomposition rates. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "peatDrainageProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peat Drainage Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-peatVolumeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Peat Volume Property quantifies the proportion of peat material in a given soil volume. It is critical for land use planning, carbon sequestration assessments, and wetland management. Source: Soil Survey Staff (2014). Keys to Soil Taxonomy." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "peatVolumeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peat Volume Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-plasticityProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Plasticity Property measures the soil's ability to deform under stress without cracking or breaking, especially when wet. It is influenced by clay content and mineralogy and is important for engineering and agricultural applications. Source: Hillel, D. (2004). Introduction to Environmental Soil Physics." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "plasticityProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Plasticity Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-poresAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Pores Abundance Property describes the quantity of soil pores, ranging from few to many. It influences water infiltration, root growth, and soil aeration. Pore abundance is crucial for assessing soil health and productivity. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "poresAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pores Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-poresSizeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Pores Size Property categorizes soil pores by their diameter, such as macro-pores, meso-pores, and micro-pores. This property determines water retention, air movement, and root penetration in soil. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "poresSizeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pores Size Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-porosityClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Porosity Class Property classifies soil by the proportion of pore spaces, typically expressed as a percentage. Classes include high, medium, or low porosity, influencing water and air movement in the soil. Source: Soil Science Society of America (SSSA) Glossary." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "porosityClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Porosity Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-profileDescriptionStatusProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Profile Description Status Property indicates the level of detail and accuracy in documenting the features of a soil profile, including its horizons, texture, structure, and other observable characteristics. This property ensures that the soil profile description meets standards for completeness, enabling reliable classification and analysis. It is a critical aspect of soil studies, providing the basis for land-use planning, environmental assessment, and agricultural decision-making. Source: Schoeneberger, P.J., Wysocki, D.A., Benham, E.C., & Soil Survey Staff. (2012). Field Book for Describing and Sampling Soils. USDA-NRCS." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "profileDescriptionStatusProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Profile Description Status Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-rockAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Rock Abundance property refers to the proportion or percentage of rock fragments in the soil profile. High rock abundance can affect soil tillage, water retention, root penetration, and overall soil fertility. It is often used to assess land suitability for agriculture and vegetation. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description (4th ed.). Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "rockAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rock Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-rockOutcropsCoverProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Rock Outcrops Cover Property measures the extent to which rock outcrops (exposed bedrock or rock masses) cover the landscape. The presence of rock outcrops can influence soil depth and water retention, as well as contribute to erosion and landscape stability, particularly in areas with minimal soil cover. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Soil Survey Manual'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "rockOutcropsCoverProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rock Outcrops Cover Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-rockOutcropsDistanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Rock Outcrops Distance Property quantifies the distance from a given point or area to the nearest rock outcrop. The proximity to rock outcrops can influence soil development, with areas closer to outcrops often having thinner soils and higher vulnerability to erosion. Source: FAO, 'Soil Formation and Classification'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "rockOutcropsDistanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rock Outcrops Distance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-rockShapeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Rock Shape property describes the physical form of rock fragments in the soil, which can range from angular to rounded. Angular rocks are generally a sign of recent weathering, while rounded rocks have been smoothed by long-term weathering processes. The shape of rocks influences soil texture, drainage, and tillage requirements. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description (4th ed.). Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "rockShapeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rock Shape Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-rockSizeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Rock Size property refers to the dimensions of the rock fragments present in the soil. Rocks are typically classified by their size, from small gravel to large boulders. Larger rocks can limit root growth, hinder water infiltration, and complicate soil management practices. Source: Soil Science Society of America. (2008). Soil Science Glossary. Soil Science Society of America." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "rockSizeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rock Size Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-rootsAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Roots Abundance Property describes the quantity of plant roots within a soil profile, typically categorized as few, common, or many. Root abundance reflects plant-soil interactions, soil fertility, and the physical structure that supports vegetation. Source: Soil Survey Manual (USDA, 2017)." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "rootsAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Roots Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-saltContentProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Salt Content Property measures the concentration of soluble salts in soil, critical for assessing salinity. Excess salts can hinder plant growth by affecting osmotic potential, leading to reduced water uptake by roots. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "saltContentProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Salt Content Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-saltCoverProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Salt cover refers to the accumulation of salts on the surface of the soil, often visible as a crystalline crust. This phenomenon occurs when capillary action causes soluble salts to move upwards through the soil, where they crystallize as water evaporates. Salt cover can interfere with seed germination, plant growth, and water infiltration, and is commonly found in arid and semi-arid regions with high evaporation rates. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils (14th ed.). Pearson Education. " ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "saltCoverProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Salt Cover Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-saltPresenceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The salt presence property of soil refers to the concentration of soluble salts, such as sodium, calcium, and magnesium, within the soil profile. High salt levels can lead to soil salinity, negatively affecting plant growth by altering water availability and soil structure. The presence of salts is typically measured using electrical conductivity (EC) or specific ion concentration tests. Soils with high salt presence are often found in arid regions where evaporation exceeds precipitation, leading to salt accumulation at or near the surface. Source: Rhoades, J.D., & Kandiah, A. (1992). Soil Salinity Management in Agriculture. Springer. " ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "saltPresenceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Salt Presence Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-saltProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Salt Property encompasses all forms of salts present in soil, including sodium chloride, calcium sulfate, and magnesium sulfate. It influences soil chemistry, structure, and plant tolerance to salinity. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "saltProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Salt Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-sandyTextureProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Sandy Texture Property refers to soil dominated by sand particles (0.05–2.0 mm in diameter). Sandy soils are well-drained, low in nutrients, and prone to drought due to poor water retention. Source: Soil Science Society of America (SSSA) Glossary." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "sandyTextureProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sandy Texture Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-sealingConsistenceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The sealing consistence property of soil describes the resistance of the soil to the formation of a surface crust or compacted layer, which can impede water infiltration and root penetration. This phenomenon typically occurs in fine-textured soils, such as clays and silts, where the particles are tightly bound, leading to a hardened surface layer upon wetting and drying. Sealing consistence affects water movement and can lead to surface runoff and erosion in agricultural soils. Source: Soil Science Society of America (SSSA). (2008). Glossary of Soil Science Terms. Soil Science Society of America. " ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "sealingConsistenceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sealing Consistence Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-sealingThicknessProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The sealing thickness property refers to the depth of the compacted or sealed layer within the soil, which is typically a result of intense rainfall, drying, or tillage practices. This layer, which forms in fine-textured soils, can significantly reduce water infiltration and root growth, leading to reduced soil aeration and potential surface runoff. The sealing thickness property is crucial for understanding soil erosion risks and water management in agricultural systems. Source: Pagliai, M., & Vignozzi, N. (2002). Soil Structure and Soil Quality: A Review of the State of the Art. Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment, 88(3), 77-86. " ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "sealingThicknessProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sealing Thickness Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-shrubsAbundanceProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Shrubs Abundance Property refers to the extent or abundance of shrub vegetation in a given area. Shrubs influence soil structure by reducing erosion, improving soil organic matter through leaf litter, and enhancing nutrient cycling. They are also indicators of ecosystem health in many terrestrial environments. Source: FAO, 'Vegetation and Land Use in Arid Zones'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "shrubsAbundanceProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shrubs Abundance Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-slopeFormProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Slope Form Property describes the shape or curvature of a slope, including its concave, convex, or straight profile. Slope form affects water runoff, erosion rates, and soil accumulation, influencing both the fertility and stability of the soil in hilly or mountainous regions. Source: FAO, 'Soil and Water Conservation'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "slopeFormProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Slope Form Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-slopeGradientClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Slope Gradient Class Property refers to the classification of slope steepness, such as flat, moderate, or steep, based on its angle. The gradient of a slope influences soil erosion rates, water drainage, and the suitability of land for agriculture, with steeper slopes typically being more prone to erosion. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Soil Erosion and Conservation'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "slopeGradientClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Slope Gradient Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-slopeGradientProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Slope Gradient Property refers to the numerical measurement of the steepness of a slope, usually expressed as a percentage or angle. This property is crucial in determining soil erosion potential, water runoff, and suitability for land use, as steeper slopes are more prone to erosion and less suitable for cultivation without proper soil conservation measures. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Soil Erosion and Conservation'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "slopeGradientProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Slope Gradient Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-slopeOrientationClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Slope Orientation Class Property classifies the direction in which a slope faces (north, south, east, west) and can significantly affect local climate conditions, such as sunlight exposure, moisture, and temperature. For instance, south-facing slopes in the Northern Hemisphere receive more sunlight, influencing soil temperature, vegetation types, and soil moisture availability. Source: FAO, 'Soil and Water Conservation'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "slopeOrientationClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Slope Orientation Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-slopeOrientationProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Slope Orientation Property refers to the precise directional orientation of a slope, measured in degrees from a cardinal direction (e.g., 0° for north, 180° for south). The slope orientation impacts factors such as solar radiation, evaporation rates, and vegetation cover, all of which affect soil temperature, moisture retention, and erosion potential. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Soil Erosion and Landscape Analysis'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "slopeOrientationProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Slope Orientation Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-slopePathwaysProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Slope Pathways Property identifies the natural flow paths of water, sediment, or other materials across a slope. These pathways, often defined by the topography, influence how water moves, erodes soil, and accumulates nutrients or contaminants. Understanding these pathways is critical for effective erosion control and water management in sloped areas. Source: FAO, 'Soil Erosion and Conservation'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "slopePathwaysProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Slope Pathways Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-soilDepthBedrockProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Soil Depth Bedrock property refers to the distance from the soil surface to the underlying bedrock layer. This measurement is important for understanding the soil's capacity to support plant growth, as soils with shallow bedrock tend to have limited rooting depth, affecting water retention and nutrient availability. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description (4th ed.). Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "soilDepthBedrockProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Soil Depth Bedrock Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-soilDepthProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Soil Depth property measures the vertical distance from the soil surface to an impenetrable layer, such as bedrock or a hardpan. Soil depth is a crucial factor influencing root development, water storage, and plant growth. Shallow soils may limit root growth, while deep soils can support more extensive root systems. Source: Brady, N. C., & Weil, R. R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils (14th ed.). Pearson Prentice Hall." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "soilDepthProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Soil Depth Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-soilDepthRootableClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Soil Depth Rootable Class property classifies soils based on the depth of the rootable zone, which excludes impenetrable layers such as bedrock or compacted horizons. Soils are typically classified as shallow, moderately deep, or deep, depending on the depth of the root zone, which directly affects plant growth. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description (4th ed.). Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "soilDepthRootableClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Soil Depth Rootable Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-soilDepthRootableProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Soil Depth Rootable property refers to the actual vertical depth of soil that can support plant roots, excluding any non-rootable layers such as bedrock or a water-impervious horizon. This depth is crucial for determining the suitability of land for agriculture and vegetation. Source: Soil Science Society of America. (2008). Soil Science Glossary. Soil Science Society of America." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "soilDepthRootableProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Soil Depth Rootable Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-soilDepthSampledProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Soil Depth Sampled property indicates the specific depth of the soil profile that has been sampled for analysis. This property is important for understanding the representativeness of the sample, as different soil horizons may vary significantly in properties such as texture, fertility, and moisture content. Source: Soil Science Society of America. (2008). Soil Science Glossary. Soil Science Society of America." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "soilDepthSampledProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Soil Depth Sampled Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-soilTypeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Soil Type Property categorizes soils based on their physical, chemical, and mineralogical characteristics, as well as their formation processes. It identifies specific soil types, such as sandy, loamy, or clayey soils, or more detailed classifications like Mollisols, Alfisols, or Vertisols, depending on the classification system used (e.g., USDA Soil Taxonomy or FAO WRB). Understanding soil type is essential for predicting soil behavior, determining suitable land uses, and managing resources effectively. Source: USDA-NRCS (1999). Soil Taxonomy: A Basic System of Soil Classification for Making and Interpreting Soil Surveys; FAO (2015). World Reference Base for Soil Resources. " ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "soilTypeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Soil Type Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-solubleAnionsTotalProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Soluble Anions Total Property measures the concentration of anions (e.g., chloride, sulfate, nitrate) in soil solution. High levels may indicate salinity, nutrient availability, or contamination. Source: Sparks, D.L. (2003). Environmental Soil Chemistry." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "solubleAnionsTotalProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Soluble Anions Total Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-solubleCationsTotalProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Soluble Cations Total Property evaluates the sum of cations (e.g., sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium) in soil solution. It is an indicator of soil salinity, fertility, and chemical balance. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "solubleCationsTotalProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Soluble Cations Total Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-stickinessProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Stickiness Property measures the soil’s tendency to adhere to Layer-Horizons or objects when wet. It is influenced by clay content and organic matter and affects soil workability and machinery use. Source: Hillel, D. (2004). Introduction to Environmental Soil Physics." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "stickinessProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stickiness Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-structureGradeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Structure Grade Property assesses the development and stability of soil aggregates, classified as weak, moderate, or strong. It reflects soil tilth, aeration, and resistance to erosion. Source: Soil Survey Staff (2014). Keys to Soil Taxonomy." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "structureGradeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Structure Grade Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-structureSizeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Structure Size Property describes the dimensions of soil aggregates or structural units, such as granular, blocky, or platy. Structure size affects root penetration, water infiltration, and soil stability. Source: FAO (2006). Guidelines for Soil Description." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "structureSizeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Structure Size Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-surfaceAgeProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Surface Age Property refers to the estimated age of a surface layer of soil or landform, which is important for understanding the degree of soil weathering, development, and fertility. Older surfaces may have more developed soils, while younger surfaces may be less fertile and more prone to erosion. Source: FAO, 'Soil Formation and Evolution'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "surfaceAgeProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Surface Age Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-textureLabClassProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Texture Lab Class Property defines soil texture using laboratory-determined particle size distributions. This property provides a precise classification of soil as sand, silt, or clay using standard methods. Source: Brady, N.C., & Weil, R.R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "textureLabClassProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Texture Lab Class Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-textureProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Texture property refers to the relative proportions of sand, silt, and clay in a soil. It is a key indicator of the soil’s ability to retain moisture, drain water, and support root growth. Soils can be classified into textures such as sandy, loamy, or clayey, each with distinct characteristics affecting soil management. Source: Soil Science Society of America. (2008). Soil Science Glossary. Soil Science Society of America." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "textureProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Texture Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-treeDensityProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tree Density Property refers to the number of trees per unit area, usually expressed as trees per hectare. High tree density is often associated with forested ecosystems that contribute to soil fertility through organic matter inputs, root structures that stabilize the soil, and enhanced moisture retention. Source: FAO, 'Forest Resources Assessment'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "treeDensityProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tree Density Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-voidsDiameterProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Voids Diameter Property refers to the size of void spaces in soil, which can range from macro-voids (larger pores) to micro-voids (tiny pores). Voids diameter impacts soil aeration, water retention, and drainage. Source: Soil Science Society of America (SSSA) Glossary." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "voidsDiameterProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Voids Diameter Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-weatherConditionsCurrentProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Weather Conditions Current Property describes the current atmospheric conditions in a specific location, including factors like temperature, humidity, wind speed, and precipitation. These conditions directly affect soil moisture, temperature, and erosion rates, influencing soil fertility and the ability to support plant growth. Source: USGS, 'Meteorological and Soil Interactions'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "weatherConditionsCurrentProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Weather Conditions Current Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-weatherConditionsPastProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Weather Conditions Past Property refers to historical weather data, including past patterns of temperature, precipitation, and extreme weather events. Understanding past weather conditions helps in predicting soil behavior, erosion patterns, and agricultural productivity, as well as preparing for future changes in soil moisture and temperature. Source: USDA NRCS, 'Climate and Soil Interactions'" ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "weatherConditionsPastProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Weather Conditions Past Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-weatheringFragmentsProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Weathering Fragments property refers to soil particles or rock fragments that have been broken down through physical, chemical, or biological weathering. These fragments play a role in soil texture, structure, and nutrient availability. The degree of weathering can indicate the age and stability of the soil’s mineral content. Source: Brady, N. C., & Weil, R. R. (2008). The Nature and Properties of Soils (14th ed.). Pearson Prentice Hall." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "weatheringFragmentsProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Weathering Fragments Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode-wetPlasticityProperty a glosis_cl:DescriptivePropertyCode,
        skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Wet Plasticity Property measures the soil’s ability to deform under pressure when saturated. Wet plasticity is influenced by clay mineralogy and is important for understanding soil behavior under wet conditions. Source: Hillel, D. (2004). Introduction to Environmental Soil Physics." ;
    skos:inScheme glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode ;
    skos:notation "wetPlasticityProperty" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wet Plasticity Property"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf glosis_cl:descriptivePropertyCode .

